      "author": "Tried By Fire",
      "authorHandle": "@TriedByFire",
      "text": "Solomon could have held all his wisdom in for himself, and left everyone else in the dust. But that's not what he wants to do. He wants to share the wealth of his wisdom so that it might be useful to other people to grow and find real value in life. Solomon is writing this in his old age. I imagine he is looking back on his life, and thinking about what kind of future is going to take place in Israel after he is dead and gone. He does not want his kids to make the same mistakes, or even worse mistakes, that he did. I believe he thought, \"If I write down the most important things to remember in this life, my kids will be able to look at it and have no excuse for messing anything up.\" Of course, we know that his kids WILL mess it up (if you didn't know that, then I'm sorry for the spoiler). Solomon doesn't want that though, and so he is writing down his most useful information that he has learned over the years.",
